## §4 Result model & combat log

Every resolved move records its outcome in **three orthogonal axes** on
the combat host. They are independent: an attack that missed but
triggered a counter sets the attack axis to `missed` and the retal axis
to fired, both true at once. The combat log reads all three to render
the row.

| Host var | Entry mirror | Values | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| `pod_combat_last_outcome` | `entry.var:outcome` | `flag:attack` / `heal` / `morale_damage` / `morale_healed` / `utility` | What kind of action the row was. |
| `pod_combat_last_attack_result` | `entry.var:attack_result` | `flag:hp_damage` / `soaked` / `missed` / `dodged` / `stealthed` / `guard_blocked` | How the attack resolved. **Meaningful only when `outcome = flag:attack`** — readers gate on the outcome axis first. |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` | `entry.var:retal_fired` | `1` or absent | Overlay: a counter-strike resolved. Independent of the other two. |

`push_log` captures the host vars onto the log-entry story as the entry
mirrors; the log dispatcher (`PODCombatLogResultText`) and the retal
rider customizable_loc render from those. `flag:stealthed` is kept
distinct from `flag:dodged` so the loc line links to the Stealth
concept; both auto-miss.

**Actual-result markers** ride alongside the axes, stamped from PoD's
per-victim classification rather than the move's damage class (see
**Damage pipeline**): `pod_combat_last_result_agg` →
`entry.var:result_agg` splits the main damage line's wound-box icon, and
`pod_combat_last_retal_agg` / `_last_retal_morale` →
`entry.var:retal_agg` / `retal_morale` split the retal rider's. Absent =
superficial.

### How helpers write the axes

- **Attack outcomes** use `pod_combat_set_attack_result_effect = { CODE
  = ... }`, which writes `outcome = flag:attack` **and** `attack_result
  = flag:$CODE$` together. Writing them in lockstep is why readers can
  trust that `attack_result` is meaningful whenever `outcome = attack`.
- **Secondary outcomes** (HP heal, morale damage, morale heal) use
  `pod_combat_set_outcome_if_unset_effect = { CODE = ... }`, which
  writes the outcome only if none is set yet. A damage+heal move keeps
  `attack` as its primary outcome; the heal amount rides in
  `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` and the heal rider emits a side line.
- **Retaliation** uses `pod_combat_set_retal_fired_effect`, which sets
  the overlay unconditionally. It is not a transform of the outcome flag
  — a missed main attack with a landed counter keeps `attack_result =
  missed` and signals the counter via the overlay, so neither axis loses
  information.

### Reset, sentinel, and the utility default

§2 of `POD_execute_combat_move` removes all three axis vars and the
actual-result markers (via
`pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect`). **Absence is the sentinel**
for "no result yet" — helpers write the matching axis as the move
resolves. At log-push time `push_log` defaults `entry.var:outcome` to
`flag:utility` when the host var is still unset (a move that did nothing
measurable, e.g. a pure stance or a fully-practice spar);
`attack_result` and `retal_fired` are simply not captured when their
host vars are unset.

Practice mode (sparring) returns `flag:utility` from the route helper,
so morale spillover, retaliation, and the log line all fall through to
the utility row.

### Result-code partition triggers

The rider and reaction gates compose against the axes through partition
triggers (`pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt`), so the
attack_result enum is grouped in one place. Each gates on `outcome =
flag:attack` first, so non-attack outcomes fail every partition by
design:

| Trigger | Passes on |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger` | `attack_result = hp_damage` — a wound went through. (OR'd with the `pod_combat_aoe_any_hp_damage` latch so an AoE drain heals once if **any** target took HP damage, since the per-target log clear wipes the host axes mid-loop.) |
| `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger` | `attack_result` ∈ (`hp_damage`, `guard_blocked`, `soaked`) — anything that physically touched, even if absorbed. Used by retaliate-on-contact and `on_contact` riders. |
| `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_miss_trigger` | `attack_result` ∈ (`missed`, `dodged`, `stealthed`) — the attack was evaded. Used by retaliate-on-evasion and `on_miss` riders. |

### Combat log

The combat log is a rolling feed of one row per resolved action. Each
row is a `POD_combat_log_entry` story owned by Charon
(`character:POD_wraith_01`, a guaranteed-alive anchor); the PC's
`pod_combat_log` variable list holds references to them.

#### Append-only, capped at 30

The list is **append-only** (chronological, oldest first) and capped at
`pod_combat_log_max_entries` (30). Both action rows and system rows go
through one shared helper,
`pod_combat_append_capped_log_entry_effect`, which appends the new entry
to `pod_combat_log`, stamps a per-fight `log_seq` on it, and drops the
single oldest entry (lowest `log_seq`, via `ordered_in_list`) when the
list exceeds the cap. Trimmed entries are `end_story`'d so the underlying
story doesn't leak on Charon. The GUI log `vbox` renders the list
**`bottomtotop`**, so an oldest-first list still displays newest-at-top
with no per-push list rebuild (CK3 variable lists only append, and GUI
datamodels cannot sort — the earlier design paid an O(N)-per-push
prepend rebuild to fake newest-first; `bottomtotop` moves that to the
display layer for free). `log_seq` exists only to identify the oldest
row for the cap trim; nothing else reads it.

Two callers create the entries:

- **`pod_combat_push_log_effect`** — the per-action row. Snapshots one
  resolved move: a live `move` story reference (the GUI reads the damage
  class, targeting, and the primitive flags off it), `actor` /
  `target` char refs, the amount fields (`damage`, `heal_amount`,
  `morale_damage_amount`, `morale_heal_amount`, `self_damage`,
  `retal_damage`), the three result-axis vars (`outcome` /
  `attack_result` / `retal_fired`, see **Result model**), the
  actual-result markers (`result_agg` / `retal_agg` / `retal_morale`),
  and the segment-visibility bake described below.
- **`pod_combat_push_system_log_effect`** (and the `_named` sibling,
  which also stamps `actor`) — a move-less row carrying only a
  `system_message` flag.

#### The result column

The result column is composed from **one head plus a sequence of
segments**, each separated by a ` · ` divider:

- The **head** is `PODCombatLogResultText`, a three-axis dispatcher. It
  resolves the primary line off `entry.var:outcome` and
  `entry.var:attack_result` (with the wound-box icon split on the
  entry's actual-result marker `result_agg`, so the icon matches what
  actually landed on this victim):
  hit/soaked/blocked/missed/dodged/stealthed for attacks, plus heal /
  morale-damage / morale-heal / utility lines.
- The **segments** are independent per-primitive renderers appended
  after the head, one `PODCombatLogSeg*` customizable_loc each:

| Segment | Renders when the move has |
|---------|--------------------------|
| `PODCombatLogSegRecoil` | self-damage > 0 on the entry |
| `PODCombatLogSegHitTier` | a hit buff/debuff tier |
| `PODCombatLogSegSoakTier` | a soak buff/debuff tier |
| `PODCombatLogSegGuard` | `set_guard` |
| `PODCombatLogSegDodge` | `dodge` |
| `PODCombatLogSegStealth` | `stealth` |
| `PODCombatLogSegRemoveGuard` | `remove_guard` |
| `PODCombatLogSegRetalArmed` | `retaliate_if_hit` / `retaliate_if_missed` |
| `PODCombatLogSegApGain` | `ap_gain` |
| `PODCombatLogSegApDrain` | `ap_drain` (full drain renders as Stun) |
| `PODCombatLogSegInitDebuff` | `initiative_debuff` |
| `PODCombatLogSegEnergyGain` | a fired `energy_gain` bonus (`energy_gain` > 0 on the entry) |
| `PODCombatLogSegEnergyDrain` | a fired `energy_drain` bonus (`energy_drain` > 0 on the entry) |

Separate **amount riders** (`PODCombatLogHealRider`,
`PODCombatLogMoraleDamageRider`, `PODCombatLogMoraleHealRider`,
`PODCombatLogRetalRider`) append the actual numbers for heal / morale /
counter effects that ride alongside the primary line. The retal rider
splits on the counter's actual result (morale / aggravated / superficial
/ soaked, off the entry's `retal_morale` / `retal_agg` markers),
matching the firing order in **Reactions**.

#### Visibility is baked once at push time

Segment visibility is **decided once when the row is pushed**, not
re-evaluated every UI tick. Inside `pod_combat_push_log_effect`, after
the amount and result fields are stamped, the bake walks the segments in
display order and writes two presence vars per visible segment:

- `seg_<x>_on` — the segment renders content
- `seg_<x>_sep` — a ` · ` separator precedes it (this segment is visible
  **and** the head or an earlier segment already rendered)

A running `seg_bake_prior` counter tracks whether any earlier content
exists (the head counts unless `outcome = utility`, whose loc renders
empty). The corresponding visibility script values in
`pod_combat_values.txt` — `pod_combat_log_seg_<x>_visible_value`,
`pod_combat_log_before_<x>_value`, and the `*_rider_visible_value` set —
are then **thin `has_variable` reads** of those baked vars. Entry vars
are immutable after push, so each read is a single variable check.

The bake conditions:

- **Heal / morale riders**: amount captured and `> 0`, `NAND` outcome
  equals the rider's own primary (so a pure-heal row doesn't
  double-print the heal). `NAND`, not `NOT` — `outcome` is always
  stamped, so a multi-child `NOT` (= `NOR`) would be permanently false
  and silently kill the rider.
- **Retal rider**: `retal_fired` present.
- **Move-level segments** (recoil's siblings and the intent segments):
  burst-gated via `pod_combat_log_seg_burst_gate_trigger`, which passes
  only on a burst's first row (`aoe_burst_index` = 0) or on non-AoE rows
  (no `aoe_burst_index`), so a move-level line renders once per AoE
  burst rather than once per target. Recoil is **not** burst-gated:
  self-damage is applied once to the actor (never per target), so it can
  only appear on a single-target row or the AoE wrap-up row. The
  energy bonus segments follow recoil's shape: entry-captured
  (`energy_gain` / `energy_drain`, lifted at §9b only when the bonus
  actually applied — the drain lift sits inside the practice gate), not
  burst-gated, and cleared per push so at most one row per move carries
  them.

Rows that skip the bake (system / DoT / mind / daze rows) carry no
`seg_*` vars and read as all-invisible.

#### System and bookend rows

Move-less rows carry a flag-valued `system_message` and render via
`PODCombatLogSystemMessageText`. Lifecycle bookends:

| `system_message` | Pushed when |
|------------------|-------------|
| `combat_started` | combat begins |
| `round_begins` | each new round |
| `combat_ended_victory` / `combat_ended_defeat` | combat ends (which side won) |
| `combatant_joins` | a mid-fight summon seats a combatant (named row) |
| `ally_down_shock` | a teammate is knocked out (named row) |

The same dispatcher also handles DoT (`dot_applied_*` / `dot_tick_*`),
daze/stun (`dazed` / `stunned`), and mind-control (`mind_seized` /
`mind_resisted` / `mind_betrayal` / `mind_fizzle`) rows, each pushed by
its own dedicated helper (`pod_combat_push_dot_applied_log_effect`,
`pod_combat_push_dot_tick_log_effect`,
`pod_combat_push_ap_drain_log_effect`,
`pod_combat_push_mind_log_effect`). All set `actor_side = -1` so the GUI
renders them in the neutral system lane.

#### AoE rows

An AoE move pushes **one row per target** from inside its iterator. Each
per-target row carries `aoe_burst_index` (0 for the first, 1+ after) so
the move-level segments render once via the burst gate, and `target` set
to the iterated character so the target column reads "All enemies
(Steve)". The iterator also sets the `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows`
tripwire.

After the AoE loop, the final push step gates the **wrap-up row** on
that tripwire: when the tripwire is set, the wrap-up is normally skipped
(the per-target rows already covered the fight), **unless** actor-side
state fired that the per-target rows missed — a self-heal
(`pod_combat_last_heal_amount`), self-morale-heal
(`pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount`), a retal overlay
(`pod_combat_last_retal_fired`), recoil self-damage
(`pod_combat_last_self_damage`), or a fired energy bonus
(`pod_combat_last_energy_gain` / `_drain`). In that case the wrap-up row is pushed
carrying just those riders. Non-AoE moves never set the tripwire, so
they always push exactly one row.

