### Scope-VALUE stash (numbers / booleans)

Numeric and boolean scope values ride story vars with explicit
exists-guards (`?=` can't navigate them): `provisions_loss` and
`cannibal_prowess` round-trip their numbers; `scheme_discovered`
stores a literal marker and the restore re-emits `yes`. FLAG-typed
scope values (e.g. `scope:method`) still cannot ride this — flags
degrade to plain `value` through variable copies — and stay in the
not-stashed register.

### Victor snapping

`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect` can record a SUMMON as the
winner (PC lost; the first alive enemy was a summoned combatant).
Vanilla duels only ever name the two duelists, and follow-up identity
compares (`sc_victor = scope:recidivist`, `= scope:spirit`, ...)
assume that — so the phase-1 restore snaps a non-principal victor to
the winning side's principal (the loser is always a principal, so the
victor is whichever principal the loser isn't). The snapped principal
can be dead; follow-ups already tolerate dead victors. This fixed the
diablerize / vamphunt / umbra-crisis chains in one place.

### Task-contract aliases

| Alias | Source |
|---|---|
| `task_contract` | The adventurer contract duels complete their contract DOWNSTREAM of the OUTPUT_EVENT: Hunt Criminals (`laamp_base_contract_schemes.1305` routes to `.1302`/`.1303`, which run `complete_task_contract` and read the reward gold off the contract's vars) and Aid Wanderer (`ep3_contract_event.0575` re-derives employer/taker FROM the contract via `set_contract_scopes_effect`, then completes it). Without the stash the player wins the duel and gets no reward, and the contract zombies. Typed scope; vanilla stores contracts in variables itself. |

### Adding a new alias

When a new parent event surfaces an undefined-event-target error in
its follow-up — **or in any event the follow-up chains into via
`trigger_event`; audit the whole downstream chain, not just the
OUTPUT_EVENT** — add a stash line to
`pod_combat_sc_stash_parent_aliases_effect`:

```
scope:<name> ?= { scope:sc_ctx = { set_variable = { name = alias_<name> value = scope:<name> } } }
```

…and the matching restore line in `pod_combat.9999`'s alias-rebuild
block:

```
scope:sc_ctx.var:alias_<name> ?= { save_scope_as = <name> }
```

`?=` is CK3's safe-scope-navigation operator: the body runs in the
LHS scope iff the LHS resolves, otherwise it's a silent no-op. That's
how the whitelist stays open by design — extra branches that find
no scope at launch are silent skips. (Pre-`?=` versions used
`if = { limit = { exists = scope:X } scope:X = { ... } }`; same
semantics, more syntax.)

### Surfaces NOT yet stashed

- **Typed scope objects**: `scope:vassal_war` (its caller has no
  OUTPUT_EVENT, so it is not on the intercept path today).
- **Raw scope values**: `scope:duel_legal`, `scope:fp2_*_gate`
  (exists-checks only / never set), and `scope:method` (Hunt
  Criminals `.1302` desc paragraph — blanks cosmetically). `method`
  is FLAG-typed and flags degrade through variable copies; numeric
  and boolean values are now covered by the value stash above.
- **Repurposed vanilla outputs**: the two PoD launchers that pointed
  at `tribal.1021` (the vanilla liege-challenge output, which reads
  liege-chain scopes they never set and docks the surviving victor a
  prestige level on an attacker death) now point at the dedicated
  `pod_combat.9990` aftermath event instead.

